<BaseLayout title="DEA-C01 Domain 1 Course" activeSlug="courses" description="Complete Domain 1 presentation for AWS Certified Data Engineer – Associate.">
<section class="course-shell" aria-label="DEA-C01 Domain 1 presentation">
  <div class="course-topbar"><div><a class="course-back" href={`${base}/courses/dea-c01/`}>← DEA-C01 presentations</a><p class="course-kicker">DEA-C01 · Domain 1 · 34% of the exam</p><h1>Data Ingestion and Transformation</h1></div><div class="course-actions"><span id="course-counter" aria-live="polite">Slide 1 of 29</span><button id="course-fullscreen" type="button" aria-label="Enter fullscreen">Fullscreen</button><button id="course-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous slide">←</button><button id="course-next" type="button" aria-label="Next slide">Next →</button></div></div>
  <div class="course-progress" aria-hidden="true"><div id="course-progress-fill"></div></div>
  <div class="course-deck">
    <article class="course-slide is-active"><span class="slide-number">01</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Course map</p><h2>What Domain 1 expects you to do</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Ingest</b><span>Move batch, stream, API, SaaS, and database data.</span></div><div><b>Transform</b><span>Choose engines, formats, and performance controls.</span></div><div><b>Orchestrate</b><span>Coordinate triggers, dependencies, retries, and alerts.</span></div><div><b>Engineer</b><span>Read code concepts and build reliable pipelines.</span></div></div><div class="course-callout"><strong>Exam lens:</strong> select the AWS service that fits latency, volume, replay, operational effort, resilience, and cost.</div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Domain 1 is the largest exam domain. The central skill is translating a requirement into a service choice: a continuous clickstream has different needs from a nightly database extract, even when both end in the same data lake.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">02</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Foundation</p><h2>A data pipeline is a production line</h2><div class="pipeline-diagram"><div><b>1</b><span>Source</span><small>apps, databases, devices, SaaS</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>2</b><span>Ingest</span><small>stream, batch, CDC</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>3</b><span>Process</span><small>validate, enrich, format</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>4</b><span>Serve</span><small>S3, Redshift, APIs</small></div></div><ul class="course-bullets two-up"><li><strong>Batch:</strong> data arrives in files or scheduled loads; minutes or hours are acceptable.</li><li><strong>Streaming:</strong> events arrive continuously; seconds or lower latency matters.</li><li><strong>CDC:</strong> database changes are copied while the source remains online.</li><li><strong>Raw zone:</strong> preserve the original data so that it can be reprocessed.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Every service in this domain sits at one point in this production line. The same pipeline can mix modes: DMS can continuously capture changes, Glue can process them in batches, and S3 can retain the raw input.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">03</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Decision guide</p><h2>Start with the signal in the question</h2><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>Requirement signal</th><th>First service to evaluate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Real-time events, replay, multiple independent consumers</td><td>Kinesis Data Streams</td></tr><tr><td>Managed Kafka clients, topics, offsets, Kafka tooling</td><td>Amazon MSK</td></tr><tr><td>Deliver stream to S3/Redshift with low operations</td><td>Amazon Data Firehose</td></tr><tr><td>Migration plus ongoing changes</td><td>AWS DMS full load + CDC</td></tr><tr><td>SaaS application ingestion</td><td>Amazon AppFlow</td></tr><tr><td>Serverless Spark ETL</td><td>AWS Glue</td></tr></tbody></table><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Do not select a service because its name appears familiar. The wording “minimum operational overhead” favors managed and serverless choices. “Existing Kafka ecosystem” is a deliberate clue for MSK. “Replay” is a deliberate clue for a retained stream or S3.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">04</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Streaming</p><h2>Kinesis Data Streams is a replayable event buffer</h2><div class="course-flow"><span>Producers</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">Kinesis Data Streams<br/><small>shards + retention</small></span><b>→</b><span>Lambda / Flink / consumers</span><b>→</b><span>S3 / analytics</span></div><ul class="course-bullets two-up"><li>Use for continuous, low-latency data with one or many consumers.</li><li>Records can be reread during the retention period: this is <strong>replay</strong>.</li><li>A shard is the unit of throughput and parallelism.</li><li>Use on-demand capacity for variable traffic; provision shards for predictable throughput.</li></ul><div class="course-numbers"><span><b>24h–365d</b>retention</span><span><b>10 MiB</b>max record</span><span><b>1 MB/s</b>write/shard</span><span><b>2 MB/s</b>read/shard</span></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Replay lets a repaired or new consumer read events again without asking the producer to send them again. This makes Kinesis suitable when downstream systems must process the same event independently or recover from failure.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">05</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Streaming consumers</p><h2>Standard consumers share; enhanced fan-out isolates</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>Standard consumer</h3><ul><li>Pulls with <code>GetRecords</code>.</li><li>Consumers share shard read throughput.</li><li>Use when the shared limit and pull model are acceptable.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card accent"><h3>Enhanced fan-out</h3><ul><li>Uses push delivery.</li><li>Dedicated 2 MB/s per shard per consumer.</li><li>Use for independent low-latency consumers.</li></ul></section></div><div class="course-tip"><strong>Exam cue:</strong> “dedicated throughput”, “push”, or “low latency for each consumer” means enhanced fan-out. Up to <strong>50 consumers</strong> can subscribe to a stream.</div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Think of standard consumers as sharing a single lane. Enhanced fan-out gives every consumer its own lane. Both can process the same records; the difference is throughput isolation and delivery behavior.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">06</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Streaming delivery</p><h2>Data Firehose delivers without managing consumers</h2><div class="course-flow"><span>Event producers</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">Data Firehose<br/><small>buffer · optional Lambda transform</small></span><b>→</b><span>S3 / Redshift / destinations</span></div><ul class="course-bullets"><li>Buffers data by size and time before delivery.</li><li>Can invoke Lambda for lightweight transformation before delivery.</li><li>Fits logs and events that must land in S3 or Redshift with low operational overhead.</li><li>Does <strong>not</strong> replace Kinesis Data Streams when replay or independent consumers are required.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Firehose behaves like a managed delivery channel. Once it delivers data, consumers read the destination, usually S3. It is intentionally simpler than building and operating a consumer fleet over a stream.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">07</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Kafka</p><h2>Use Amazon MSK when Kafka is the requirement</h2><div class="course-two-col"><div class="course-card"><h3>MSK fits</h3><ul><li>Existing Kafka producers and consumers.</li><li>Kafka topics, consumer groups, offsets, connectors, or schema tooling.</li><li>Long retention and Kafka processing semantics.</li></ul></div><div class="course-card"><h3>Monitor and design</h3><ul><li>Partitions create parallelism.</li><li>Consumer groups distribute partitions.</li><li>Offsets enable replay.</li><li>Keep broker CPU below 60% to preserve recovery headroom.</li></ul></div></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>MSK is managed Kafka, not a generic “better Kinesis.” If a scenario does not require Kafka compatibility, a Kinesis service is often simpler to operate in AWS-native architectures.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">08</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Database ingestion</p><h2>DMS moves a database while changes continue</h2><div class="course-flow"><span>Source database</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">DMS replication instance<br/><small>full load + CDC</small></span><b>→</b><span>Target database / S3 / Redshift</span></div><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Source endpoint</b><span>Connection details for the origin.</span></div><div><b>Target endpoint</b><span>Connection details for the destination.</span></div><div><b>Replication instance</b><span>Compute that runs replication.</span></div><div><b>Replication task</b><span>Full load, CDC, validation, mappings.</span></div></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Full load copies existing data. Change data capture reads database transaction logs and applies changes after the initial load. The combination answers migration questions where downtime must be minimal.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">09</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Batch ingestion</p><h2>S3 is the common landing zone for batch data</h2><div class="course-flow"><span>Files · APIs · SaaS</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">S3 raw zone<br/><small>immutable original records</small></span><b>→</b><span>Glue / EMR / Athena CTAS</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">S3 curated zone<br/><small>partitioned analytical data</small></span></div><ul class="course-bullets two-up"><li>S3 events can invoke Lambda, SQS, SNS, or EventBridge.</li><li>Glue crawlers infer schema and register tables and partitions.</li><li>AppFlow moves supported SaaS data with mappings, filters, and schedules.</li><li>Transfer Family, DataSync, and DMS are migration choices when the source dictates the protocol.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Keeping raw data makes a pipeline recoverable: an improved transformation can run again from the original input. The curated zone is optimized for downstream query engines, not for preserving source fidelity.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">10</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Ingestion controls</p><h2>Configure the service for the workload</h2><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Controls to recognize</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Glue ETL</td><td>Worker type, DPU count, auto scaling, job bookmarks, timeout, concurrency</td></tr><tr><td>DMS</td><td>Instance class, Multi-AZ, LOB settings, validation, full load/CDC</td></tr><tr><td>EMR</td><td>Instance fleet, core/task nodes, managed scaling, bootstrap actions</td></tr><tr><td>Redshift COPY</td><td>IAM role, format, MAXERROR, region, statistics update</td></tr><tr><td>Lambda</td><td>Memory, timeout, ephemeral storage, concurrency, batch size</td></tr></tbody></table><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Questions often present a performance or cost symptom and ask for a configuration change. The right answer changes the control owned by the named service, rather than introducing an unrelated service.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">11</span><p class="course-eyebrow">APIs and schedules</p><h2>Ingest external data safely and predictably</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>Data APIs</h3><ul><li>Use SDKs to call REST, GraphQL, or SOAP APIs.</li><li>Store keys in Secrets Manager.</li><li>Use SQS to buffer high-throughput API ingestion.</li><li>Apply exponential backoff with jitter for rate limits.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card accent"><h3>Scheduling</h3><ul><li>EventBridge Scheduler supports cron, rate, time zones, retries, and DLQs.</li><li>MWAA supports DAG schedules and backfills.</li><li>Glue triggers run jobs and crawlers on schedules or conditions.</li></ul></section></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Scheduler answers “when should this start?” A queue answers “how do we smooth a burst?” A secret manager answers “where should the credential live?” Each requirement maps to a separate responsibility.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">12</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Events</p><h2>Fan-in, fan-out, and event routing</h2><div class="pipeline-diagram"><div><b>A</b><span>Many producers</span><small>fan-in</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>B</b><span>Stream / topic / bus</span><small>durable decoupling</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>C</b><span>Many consumers</span><small>fan-out</small></div></div><ul class="course-bullets two-up"><li><strong>Kinesis:</strong> enhanced fan-out for dedicated stream consumption.</li><li><strong>Kafka:</strong> separate consumer groups.</li><li><strong>SNS:</strong> publish once to multiple SQS, Lambda, HTTP subscribers.</li><li><strong>EventBridge:</strong> match events and route them to multiple targets; archive and replay events.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Fan-in is many sources entering one logical channel. Fan-out is one event reaching several independent destinations. The best implementation depends on whether consumers need queues, event filtering, stream replay, or Kafka behavior.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">13</span><p class="course-eyebrow">State</p><h2>Stateless work scales easily; stateful work needs care</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>Stateless</h3><ul><li>Map, filter, project, and format each record independently.</li><li>Parallelize freely.</li><li>Examples: rename a field or remove null records.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card accent"><h3>Stateful</h3><ul><li>Aggregations, joins, deduplication, sessionization, windows.</li><li>Needs checkpoints and a state store.</li><li>Set state TTL to prevent unbounded growth.</li></ul></section></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>A stream processor cannot calculate a session or deduplicate a customer event without remembering earlier records. That memory is state. Reliability depends on checkpointing it and handling late-arriving events intentionally.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">14</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Transformation engines</p><h2>Select the compute engine, not just the familiar service</h2><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Best fit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AWS Glue</td><td>Serverless Spark ETL, Data Catalog integration, managed data lake pipelines</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon EMR</td><td>Large or customized Spark/Hadoop/Hive/Flink workloads and open-source control</td></tr><tr><td>AWS Lambda</td><td>Small, event-driven, short-lived transformations</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon Redshift</td><td>SQL transformations inside the warehouse</td></tr><tr><td>Containers / Batch</td><td>Specialized runtime or containerized processing requirements</td></tr></tbody></table><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Glue is usually the low-operations answer for Spark ETL. EMR becomes compelling when the question explicitly needs open-source framework control, custom cluster behavior, or a specialized big-data runtime.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">15</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Data formats</p><h2>Columnar formats reduce scan cost</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>CSV and JSON</h3><ul><li>Human-readable and common at ingestion.</li><li>Row-oriented and frequently expensive for analytics.</li><li>Useful as source formats, not always the final analytical format.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card accent"><h3>Parquet and ORC</h3><ul><li>Columnar, compressed, and optimized for analytics.</li><li>Support predicate pushdown and column pruning.</li><li>Work well with Athena, Glue, EMR, and Redshift Spectrum.</li></ul></section></div><div class="course-flow slim"><span>Raw JSON</span><b>→</b><span>partition by date / region</span><b>→</b><span>Parquet + compression</span><b>→</b><span>lower scan cost</span></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>If a query needs two columns from one day, a partitioned Parquet dataset can skip unrelated days and columns. This reduces both time and the amount of data scanned by serverless query engines.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">16</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Performance</p><h2>Partitioning, pushdown, and file layout matter</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Partition</b><span>Organize S3 paths by commonly filtered values, such as date.</span></div><div><b>Prune</b><span>Read only the partitions that a query needs.</span></div><div><b>Push down</b><span>Filter at the source instead of after moving all data.</span></div><div><b>Avoid small files</b><span>Too many tiny files create scheduling and metadata overhead.</span></div></div><div class="course-tip">For Spark, avoid <code>collect()</code> on large data; use built-in functions, broadcast joins for small lookup data, and deliberate repartitioning to mitigate skew.</div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>These techniques are related. A good storage layout lets the engine avoid work before it starts. Adding more compute without fixing layout is often a more expensive and less effective answer.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">17</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Troubleshooting</p><h2>Recognize common transformation failures</h2><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely investigation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Job runs out of memory</td><td>Driver/executor sizing, data skew, wide shuffle, unnecessary collect</td></tr><tr><td>Slow job or query</td><td>Small files, missing partitions, no pushdown, poor join strategy</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong output schema</td><td>Schema mismatch, crawler policy, source drift, partition mismatch</td></tr><tr><td>Cannot read source</td><td>IAM permissions, network path, security groups, connection configuration</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="course-caption">Glue: CloudWatch Logs, job metrics, Spark UI. EMR: step debugging and Spark History Server. Redshift: EXPLAIN and system views.</p><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Service observability is part of the expected skill. The answer should start from the tool that exposes the failure: logs for a failed job, Spark UI for execution behavior, and query plans for warehouse performance.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">18</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Modern processing</p><h2>Volume, velocity, variety, and LLM enrichment</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Volume</b><span>TB/PB workloads favor S3, EMR, Glue, or Redshift.</span></div><div><b>Velocity</b><span>Kinesis, MSK, and streaming processors handle continuous events.</span></div><div><b>Variety</b><span>Glue manages structured and semi-structured formats.</span></div><div><b>LLM processing</b><span>Bedrock can classify, extract, summarize, or normalize unstructured records.</span></div></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>The examination scope includes LLM integration for data processing, not model training. A practical pipeline can use a foundation model to turn documents into structured fields, then store those fields in the same governed data flow.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">19</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Orchestration</p><h2>Orchestration coordinates work; it does not do the work</h2><div class="pipeline-diagram"><div><b>1</b><span>Trigger</span><small>schedule or event</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>2</b><span>Orchestrator</span><small>workflow decisions</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>3</b><span>Jobs</span><small>Glue, EMR, Lambda</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>4</b><span>Alert</span><small>SNS / SQS / event</small></div></div><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>When to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Step Functions</td><td>AWS-native serverless workflow with retries, branches, Map, Parallel, and visual history</td></tr><tr><td>Amazon MWAA</td><td>Complex Airflow DAGs, custom Python operators, backfills, SLAs</td></tr><tr><td>Glue Workflows</td><td>Glue-centric jobs and crawlers</td></tr><tr><td>EventBridge</td><td>Event routing and decoupled targets</td></tr></tbody></table><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>A scheduler can begin a workflow, but an orchestrator tracks dependencies and failure paths across its steps. This distinction is useful when a question gives a multi-stage pipeline with conditional outcomes.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">20</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Step Functions</p><h2>Build failure handling into the workflow</h2><div class="course-flow"><span>Start</span><b>→</b><span>Glue job</span><b>→</b><span>Quality check</span><b>→</b><span>Publish</span></div><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Choice</b><span>Branch from input or job output.</span></div><div><b>Map / Parallel</b><span>Process files or branches concurrently.</span></div><div><b>Retry / Catch</b><span>Handle transient and terminal errors.</span></div><div><b>Wait</b><span>Pause until a time or external condition.</span></div></div><p class="course-caption">Standard workflows support long-lived, auditable executions; Express workflows favor high-volume, short-lived execution.</p><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Step Functions makes retry policy explicit rather than burying it in application code. That visibility is valuable for data pipelines that must record exactly what failed, where it failed, and what was retried.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">21</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Reliability</p><h2>Make retries safe and failures isolated</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Idempotency</b><span>Retrying a write does not create duplicates.</span></div><div><b>Checkpointing</b><span>Record the last successfully processed position.</span></div><div><b>Backoff + jitter</b><span>Retry without creating a synchronized overload.</span></div><div><b>DLQ</b><span>Isolate repeated failures for later inspection.</span></div></div><div class="course-flow"><span>Source</span><b>→</b><span>Queue / stream</span><b>→</b><span>Consumer retry</span><b>→</b><span class="aws-box">DLQ on terminal failure</span></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Retries are only safe when processing is idempotent or the destination supports transactional behavior. A dead-letter queue protects healthy records from being blocked by one malformed record or unavailable dependency.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">22</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Rate limits</p><h2>Use buffering and controlled concurrency</h2><ul class="course-bullets two-up"><li><strong>Kinesis throttling:</strong> increase shards, aggregate small records, and back off producers.</li><li><strong>API limits:</strong> use retries with exponential backoff and jitter.</li><li><strong>Lambda pressure:</strong> use reserved concurrency to cap a function and SQS to absorb bursts.</li><li><strong>Database connection storms:</strong> use RDS Proxy or a pooling pattern.</li><li><strong>S3 request rates:</strong> distribute key prefixes when a workload requires it.</li><li><strong>DynamoDB:</strong> use on-demand or correctly provisioned capacity and SDK backoff.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Rate limiting is not simply an error to retry faster. The pipeline should absorb bursts, limit concurrency toward a fragile dependency, and retry gradually. Queues create a practical buffer between those two rates.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">23</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Lambda</p><h2>Lambda is powerful, but it is not a Spark cluster</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>Good fit</h3><ul><li>S3 event reaction, API calls, small record transforms.</li><li>Manifest creation, schema registration, notification, cleanup.</li><li>Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, SQS, and EventBridge triggers.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card accent"><h3>Controls</h3><ul><li>Memory also changes CPU allocation.</li><li>Reserved concurrency protects downstream systems.</li><li>Provisioned concurrency limits cold starts.</li><li>Use EFS or up to 10 GB ephemeral storage when needed.</li></ul></section></div><div class="course-tip">Default account concurrency is <strong>1,000</strong>. Synchronous functions can scale by <strong>1,000 concurrent executions every 10 seconds</strong>.</div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Lambda is an excellent glue service between events and small units of work. Heavy joins, large shuffles, and long-running ETL signal Glue or EMR instead. The exam rewards matching the execution model to the workload.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">24</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Engineering practice</p><h2>Reliable pipelines are software products</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>Version control</b><span>Use Git and review changes before release.</span></div><div><b>Tests</b><span>Unit, integration, and data-quality assertions.</span></div><div><b>Observability</b><span>Structured logs, metrics, alarms, and correlation IDs.</span></div><div><b>Infrastructure as code</b><span>CloudFormation, CDK, or SAM make infrastructure repeatable.</span></div></div><div class="course-flow"><span>Source repository</span><b>→</b><span>Build + test</span><b>→</b><span>Deploy dev</span><b>→</b><span>Promote to production</span></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Domain 1 includes programming and engineering concepts because data pipelines are maintained systems. Versioned jobs, automated tests, controlled deployments, and useful logs reduce the risk of breaking data consumers.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">25</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Distributed computing</p><h2>Partitions, shuffles, and skew explain many Spark questions</h2><div class="pipeline-diagram"><div><b>1</b><span>Partition input</span><small>parallel tasks</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>2</b><span>Transform locally</span><small>map / filter</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>3</b><span>Shuffle if needed</span><small>join / aggregate</small></div><i>→</i><div><b>4</b><span>Write output</span><small>partitioned dataset</small></div></div><ul class="course-bullets"><li><strong>Repartition:</strong> redistribute data, often with a full shuffle.</li><li><strong>Coalesce:</strong> reduce partitions with less movement when appropriate.</li><li><strong>Broadcast join:</strong> send a small lookup table to workers to avoid a large shuffle.</li><li><strong>Skew:</strong> one key has disproportionate data, making one task much slower.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Distributed engines gain speed by working in parallel. A shuffle moves data between workers and is expensive. Good partitioning and broadcast joins reduce unnecessary network movement and are standard answers to Spark performance scenarios.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">26</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Programming concepts</p><h2>Read the behavior, not language syntax</h2><div class="course-two-col"><section class="course-card"><h3>Recognize</h3><ul><li>Loops process a collection of records.</li><li>Conditions route success and error paths.</li><li>Functions package a reusable operation.</li><li>JSON commonly represents API and event payloads.</li></ul></section><section class="course-card"><h3>Look for in scenarios</h3><ul><li>Pagination and batching.</li><li>Input validation and missing values.</li><li>Retries, error handling, and logging.</li><li>Duplicate prevention and safe writes.</li></ul></section></div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>The exam does not require language-specific mastery. It tests whether an engineer understands control flow, data structures, error paths, and performance implications well enough to diagnose or design a pipeline.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">27</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Exam patterns</p><h2>Eliminate answers by requirements</h2><table class="course-table"><thead><tr><th>If the question requires...</th><th>Reject answers that...</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Replay</td><td>Use a delivery-only service after data has left its buffer</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum operations</td><td>Build and manage clusters without an explicit requirement</td></tr><tr><td>Near-zero migration downtime</td><td>Require the source database to stop</td></tr><tr><td>Large ETL transformations</td><td>Put all processing in a short-lived Lambda function</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple independent destinations</td><td>Tie every consumer to one shared, unbuffered process</td></tr></tbody></table><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>A correct service alone is not enough. The answer must satisfy every stated constraint. Use the constraints to eliminate choices that are technically possible but too operationally heavy, not replayable, or unable to meet the latency target.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">28</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Knowledge check</p><h2>Can you explain these choices?</h2><ol class="course-questions"><li>Events must land in S3 with minimal operations and no replay requirement.<details><summary>Answer</summary><strong>Amazon Data Firehose.</strong> It buffers and delivers streaming data to managed destinations with low operational effort.</details></li><li>A production database remains online while existing rows and later changes are copied.<details><summary>Answer</summary><strong>AWS DMS full load + CDC.</strong> It copies the current data and captures ongoing transaction-log changes.</details></li><li>Daily S3 files require serverless Spark ETL and catalog integration.<details><summary>Answer</summary><strong>AWS Glue.</strong> Glue is a managed, serverless ETL service integrated with the Glue Data Catalog.</details></li><li>One event must feed analytics and fraud detection at low latency with isolated read throughput.<details><summary>Answer</summary><strong>Kinesis Data Streams with enhanced fan-out.</strong> Each consumer receives dedicated throughput.</details></li></ol><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>For every answer, state the requirement phrase that made the decision. This practice trains service selection rather than pure memorization and makes unfamiliar question wording easier to handle.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">29</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Final review</p><h2>Domain 1 readiness checklist</h2><ul class="course-bullets checklist"><li>I can distinguish streaming, batch, CDC, fan-in, fan-out, buffering, and replay.</li><li>I can choose Kinesis Data Streams, Data Firehose, MSK, DMS, AppFlow, Glue, EMR, Lambda, and Redshift for their intended patterns.</li><li>I can explain Parquet, partitioning, pushdown, small-file overhead, state, checkpoints, retries, and DLQs.</li><li>I can select EventBridge, Step Functions, MWAA, and Glue Workflows by orchestration need.</li><li>I can recognize engineering, Lambda concurrency, and distributed-computing concepts in a scenario.</li></ul><div class="course-callout"><strong>Next:</strong> use the DEA-C01 study guide and the practice exam to turn this presentation into timed scenario practice.</div><details open><summary>Additional context</summary><p>This presentation provides the foundation for the complete Domain 1 scope. Strong performance comes from repeatedly connecting requirements to service capabilities and then validating that choice with scenario questions.</p></details></article>

    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">30</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Deep dive</p><h2>Choose the ingestion path by operational boundary</h2><table class="course-table"><tr><th>Source signal</th><th>Primary service</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>SFTP/FTPS/AS2 partner files</td><td>Transfer Family</td><td>Managed protocol endpoint into S3 or EFS</td></tr><tr><td>On-premises file movement</td><td>DataSync</td><td>Accelerated, scheduled file transfer</td></tr><tr><td>SaaS records and mappings</td><td>AppFlow</td><td>Managed connectors, filters, scheduling</td></tr><tr><td>Database full load + changes</td><td>DMS</td><td>CDC without repeated full copies</td></tr></table><div class="course-tip"><strong>Trap:</strong> protocol, change capture, and event streaming are different requirements. Do not choose Kinesis just because data moves continuously.</div><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>The first question is where the data starts and what interface it exposes. The correct ingestion tool usually preserves that boundary while minimizing custom infrastructure.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">31</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Deep dive</p><h2>Make transformations efficient before adding compute</h2><div class="course-two-col"><div class="course-card"><h3>Reduce input</h3><ul><li>Filter early and project only needed columns.</li><li>Partition data for pruning.</li><li>Use predicate pushdown where supported.</li></ul></div><div class="course-card"><h3>Reduce work</h3><ul><li>Use Parquet or ORC and compression.</li><li>Avoid small files; compact outputs.</li><li>Choose joins, partitions, and workers from observed skew.</li></ul></div></div><ul class="course-bullets"><li><strong>Glue bookmarks</strong> prevent reprocessing prior inputs where incremental processing is required.</li><li><strong>Job parameters</strong> make environments and date windows repeatable.</li><li><strong>Dead-letter and retry paths</strong> make failures observable rather than silently dropping records.</li></ul><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Exam scenarios often offer more DPUs as a tempting answer. First verify that the job is reading less data, using the right format, and distributing work evenly.</p></details></article>
    <article class="course-slide"><span class="slide-number">32</span><p class="course-eyebrow">Exam strategy</p><h2>Read the requirement in this order</h2><div class="course-four-grid"><div><b>1. Latency</b><span>Seconds, minutes, or overnight?</span></div><div><b>2. Delivery</b><span>Replay, fan-out, CDC, or one destination?</span></div><div><b>3. Processing</b><span>Small event action or distributed ETL?</span></div><div><b>4. Operations</b><span>Managed, serverless, or explicit control?</span></div></div><ol class="course-questions"><li>A stream needs separate fraud and analytics consumers.<details><summary>Answer</summary>Kinesis Data Streams; use enhanced fan-out when dedicated low-latency throughput is required.</details></li><li>A daily archive needs serverless transformations and catalog integration.<details><summary>Answer</summary>Glue reading S3 and writing partitioned columnar output.</details></li><li>A source database cannot be stopped during migration.<details><summary>Answer</summary>DMS full load plus CDC.</details></li></ol><details><summary>Additional context</summary><p>Service names are less important than the requirement mapping. Eliminate each answer that fails a single required constraint before comparing cost or preference.</p></details></article>
  </div>
</section>
